deer park|[sound:3-17a.mp3]\n{ri dwags kyi nags/} Deer park, forest of wild animals\nSyllables: {ri dwags/} = wild animals with hooves; {kyi/} = {'brel sgra/}; {nags/} = forest, as in: {shing nags/} = forest, jungle, {nags tshal/} = forest, jungle\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-17b.mp3]\n {sangs rgyas kyis thengs ma dang po chos gsung sa ni lha khang ma yin par byas/_ri dwags kyi nags yin pa de nga la dpe yag po mthong gi 'dug}  I love the fact that the place the Buddha first taught was not a temple but a deer park. (H)
forest of wild animals|[sound:3-17a.mp3]\n{ri dwags kyi nags/} Deer park, forest of wild animals\nSyllables: {ri dwags/} = wild animals with hooves; {kyi/} = {'brel sgra/}; {nags/} = forest, as in: {shing nags/} = forest, jungle, {nags tshal/} = forest, jungle\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-17b.mp3]\n {sangs rgyas kyis thengs ma dang po chos gsung sa ni lha khang ma yin par byas/_ri dwags kyi nags yin pa de nga la dpe yag po mthong gi 'dug}  I love the fact that the place the Buddha first taught was not a temple but a deer park. (H)
retinue of the five excellent disciples|[sound:3-18a.mp3]\n{'khor lnga sde bzang po/} Retinue of the five excellent disciples\nSyllables: {'khor/} = retinue, as in: {'khor lo/} = wheel, {dus 'khor dbang chen/} = Kālacakra initiation; {lnga/} = five, as in: {tshes pa bco lnga/} = the 15th day of the Tibetan (lunar) month, full moon; {sde/} = set, school, faction, as in: {mdo sde/} = sūtra, set of teachings; {bzang po/} = kind, positive, as in: {blo bzang /} = kind minded, {sems pa bzang po/} = kind hearted\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-18b.mp3]\n {ri dwags kyi nags la sangs rgyas kyis gsungs pa'i chos gsan mkhan dang po de tsho la 'khor lnga sde bzang po ze lab kyi red/}  The ones who first listened to the Dharma the Buddha taught at the Deer Park are known as “the retinue of the five excellent disciples”. (H)
the three turnings of the wheel of dharma|[sound:3-19a.mp3]\n{chos kyi 'khor lo rim pa gsum/} The three turnings of the wheel of dharma\nSyllables: {chos/} = Dharma – comes from: {'chos pa/} = to make, to repair, as in: {chos lugs/} = religious tradition; {kyi/} = {'brel sgra}; {'khor lo/} = wheel, as in: {rlung 'khor/} = fan, ventilator; {rim pa/} = stages, as in: {sgom rim/} = The Stages of Meditation (by Kamalaśīla), {lam rim/} = Stages of the Path (by Tsongkapa); {gsum/} = three, as in: {dkon mchog gsum/} = The Three Jewels\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-19b.mp3]\n {chos kyi 'khor lo rim pa gsum gyi nang nas drang don dang nges don ga gi yin min gyi skor rtsod pa byung yod red/}  There are debates about which of the three turnings of the wheel of the dharma are of provisional or definitive meaning.
