the one who practice|[sound:3-277a.mp3]\n{nyams su bzhes mkhan/} The one(s) who practice(s) (H)\nSyllables: {nyams su bzhes pa/} = to practice, to apply (H), as in: {nyams myong /} = experience; {su/} = {la don/}; {bzhes pa/} = to take (H); {mkhan/} = nominalizer\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-277b.mp3]\n {deng sang yang nyams su bzhes mkhan kha shas lo mang po'i ring la dben pa'i sa cha dper na brag phug gcig gi nang logs la sgom skyon byas bzhugs kyi yod red/}  Even nowadays there are those who practice by staying in a solitary place such as a cave for many years, doing meditation. (H)
noble bodhisattva|[sound:3-278a.mp3]\n{byang sems 'phags pa/} Noble bodhisattva(s)\nSyllables: {byang /} = to be purified, as in: {byang chub/} = enlightenment; {sems/} = heart, mind, as in: {sems pa bzang po/} = kind hearted, kind minded, {gtso sems/} = main mind; {'phags pa/} = Noble One, Ārya, as in: {'phags pa'i bden pa bzhi/} = the Four Truths of the Noble Ones, {'phags pa shing kun/} = Swayambhunath\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-278b.mp3]\n {byang sems 'phags pa zer yag de su la go gi yod red zer na sa la gnas pa'i byang chub sems dpa' dang sems dpa' mo rnam pa tsho la go gi yod red/}  What does “noble bodhisattva” refer to? It refers to male and female bodhisattvas who abide on the bhūmis (bodhisattva stages). (H)
the bodhisattvas abiding on the bhūmis|[sound:3-279a.mp3]\n{sa la gnas pa'i byang chub sems dpa'/} The bodhisattvas abiding on the bhūmis, grounds\nSyllables: {sa/} = bhūmi, place, earth, as in: {sa chu me rlung nam mkha'/} = earth, water, fire, wind and space; {lha sa/} = place of the god(s); {la/} = {la don/}; {gnas pa/} = to abide, as in: {gnas stangs/} = situation, {zhi gnas/} = calm abiding; {'i/} = {'brel sgra/}; {byang chub/} = enlightenment; {byang /} = purity, purified; {chub/} = understood; {sems/} = mind, heart, as in: {sems tsam pa/} = Cittamātrin, {sems pa bzang po/} = kind hearted; {dpa'/} as in: {dpa' bo/} = hero, {dpa' mo/} = heroine\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-279b.mp3]\n {ngas sa la gnas pa'i byang chub sems dpa' zer yag de thengs ma mang po go myong yod/_yin na'i da lta bar du sa zer yag de ga re la go gi yod med gsal po byas nas ha go ma song /}  I have heard “the bodhisattvas who abide on the bhūmis” many times, but until now, I had not understood clearly what “bhūmi” refers to.
grounds|[sound:3-279a.mp3]\n{sa la gnas pa'i byang chub sems dpa'/} The bodhisattvas abiding on the bhūmis, grounds\nSyllables: {sa/} = bhūmi, place, earth, as in: {sa chu me rlung nam mkha'/} = earth, water, fire, wind and space; {lha sa/} = place of the god(s); {la/} = {la don/}; {gnas pa/} = to abide, as in: {gnas stangs/} = situation, {zhi gnas/} = calm abiding; {'i/} = {'brel sgra/}; {byang chub/} = enlightenment; {byang /} = purity, purified; {chub/} = understood; {sems/} = mind, heart, as in: {sems tsam pa/} = Cittamātrin, {sems pa bzang po/} = kind hearted; {dpa'/} as in: {dpa' bo/} = hero, {dpa' mo/} = heroine\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-279b.mp3]\n {ngas sa la gnas pa'i byang chub sems dpa' zer yag de thengs ma mang po go myong yod/_yin na'i da lta bar du sa zer yag de ga re la go gi yod med gsal po byas nas ha go ma song /}  I have heard “the bodhisattvas who abide on the bhūmis” many times, but until now, I had not understood clearly what “bhūmi” refers to.
the ten grounds|[sound:3-280a.mp3]\n{sa bcu/} The ten grounds, bhūmi, ten stages, ten bodhisattva stages\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-280b.mp3]\n {byang chub sems dpa' rnams kyi sa bcu zer yag de nyams su bzhes yag gi rim pa bcu tham pa la go gi yod red/_sa dang po yi ming ni rab tu dga' ba ze zhu gi yod red/}  When we say “the ten bodhisattva bhūmis”, it refers to ten stages of practice. (H) The first stage is called “complete joy”. (H)
bhūmi|[sound:3-280a.mp3]\n{sa bcu/} The ten grounds, bhūmi, ten stages, ten bodhisattva stages\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-280b.mp3]\n {byang chub sems dpa' rnams kyi sa bcu zer yag de nyams su bzhes yag gi rim pa bcu tham pa la go gi yod red/_sa dang po yi ming ni rab tu dga' ba ze zhu gi yod red/}  When we say “the ten bodhisattva bhūmis”, it refers to ten stages of practice. (H) The first stage is called “complete joy”. (H)
ten stages|[sound:3-280a.mp3]\n{sa bcu/} The ten grounds, bhūmi, ten stages, ten bodhisattva stages\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-280b.mp3]\n {byang chub sems dpa' rnams kyi sa bcu zer yag de nyams su bzhes yag gi rim pa bcu tham pa la go gi yod red/_sa dang po yi ming ni rab tu dga' ba ze zhu gi yod red/}  When we say “the ten bodhisattva bhūmis”, it refers to ten stages of practice. (H) The first stage is called “complete joy”. (H)
ten bodhisattva stages|[sound:3-280a.mp3]\n{sa bcu/} The ten grounds, bhūmi, ten stages, ten bodhisattva stages\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-280b.mp3]\n {byang chub sems dpa' rnams kyi sa bcu zer yag de nyams su bzhes yag gi rim pa bcu tham pa la go gi yod red/_sa dang po yi ming ni rab tu dga' ba ze zhu gi yod red/}  When we say “the ten bodhisattva bhūmis”, it refers to ten stages of practice. (H) The first stage is called “complete joy”. (H)
the five paths|[sound:3-281a.mp3]\n{lam lnga/} The Five Paths\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-281b.mp3]\n {nga tsho'i rgan lags kyi dgongs tshul la nang pa yin pa'i cha nas lam lnga po ga re ga re yin pa nges par du blo 'dzin byed dgos kyi yod red/}  In our dear teacher’s opinion, if one is a Buddhist, one certainly needs to memorize what the Five Paths are. (H)
the path of accumulation|[sound:3-282a.mp3]\n{tshogs lam/} The Path of Accumulation\nSyllables: {tshogs/} = gathering, accumulation, as in: {tshogs 'du/} = meeting; {lam/} = path, as in: {mgyogs lam/} = shortcut\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-282b.mp3]\n {ngas tshogs lam de ga re yin min gtan gtan ha ma go na yang ming dran yag las sla po 'dug ga_gang yin zer na tshogs zer yag de bsod nams kyi tshogs dang ye shes kyi tshogs dang gcig pa red/}  Even if I don’t know exactly what the “Path of Accumulation” is, it’s easy to remember the name, right? Because “{tshogs/}” (accumulation) is the same as in the accumulation of merit and the accumulation of wisdom.
the path of joining|[sound:3-283a.mp3]\n{sbyor lam/} The Path of Joining, Path of Preparation\nSyllables: {sbyor ba/} = to join together, as in: {sbyor klog} = spelling aloud; {lam/} = path, road, as in: {rgya lam/} = main road\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-283b.mp3]\n {sbyor lam zer yag de lam lnga yi nang nas gnyis pa de red/_tshogs lam tshar ba'i rjes la sbyor lam thob kyi red/_a ni de'i rjes la mthong lam la slebs kyi red/}  The “Path of Joining” is the second of the Five Paths. After the Path of Accumulation, one obtains the Path of Joining. And after that, one will reach the Path of Seeing.
path of preparation|[sound:3-283a.mp3]\n{sbyor lam/} The Path of Joining, Path of Preparation\nSyllables: {sbyor ba/} = to join together, as in: {sbyor klog} = spelling aloud; {lam/} = path, road, as in: {rgya lam/} = main road\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-283b.mp3]\n {sbyor lam zer yag de lam lnga yi nang nas gnyis pa de red/_tshogs lam tshar ba'i rjes la sbyor lam thob kyi red/_a ni de'i rjes la mthong lam la slebs kyi red/}  The “Path of Joining” is the second of the Five Paths. After the Path of Accumulation, one obtains the Path of Joining. And after that, one will reach the Path of Seeing.
the path of seeing|[sound:3-284a.mp3]\n{mthong lam/} The Path of Seeing\nSyllables: {mthong ba/} = to see, as in: {mthong chung byed pa/} = to look down upon; {lam/} = road, path, as in: {gzhung lam/} = highway\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-284b.mp3]\n {rgan lags kyis mthong lam la mthong lam zer dgos pa'i rgyu mtshan ga re red ze bka' 'dri gnang song /_don grub lags kyis gang yin zer na de dus thengs ma dang po stong nyid mngon sum du mthong yang na rtogs kyi yod red ze lan brgyab song /}  Our dear teacher asked why it is called the “Path of Seeing”. (H) {don grub lags/} responded that this was because at that point, one directly sees or realizes emptiness for the first time.
the path of cultivation|[sound:3-285a.mp3]\n{sgom lam/} The Path of Cultivation, Meditation\nSyllables: {sgom/} = meditation, as in: {bzod sgom/} = practice of patience; {lam/} = path, as in: {myur lam/} = the swift path\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-285b.mp3]\n {sgom lam la ga re byas nas ming de btags pa red zer na mthong lam skabs stong nyid mngon sum du rtogs yod pa'i nyams myong de sgom dgos red pa/}  Why has this name been given to the Path of Cultivation? Because one needs to cultivate the experience of having directly realized emptiness on the Path of Seeing, correct?
meditation|[sound:3-285a.mp3]\n{sgom lam/} The Path of Cultivation, Meditation\nSyllables: {sgom/} = meditation, as in: {bzod sgom/} = practice of patience; {lam/} = path, as in: {myur lam/} = the swift path\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-285b.mp3]\n {sgom lam la ga re byas nas ming de btags pa red zer na mthong lam skabs stong nyid mngon sum du rtogs yod pa'i nyams myong de sgom dgos red pa/}  Why has this name been given to the Path of Cultivation? Because one needs to cultivate the experience of having directly realized emptiness on the Path of Seeing, correct?
the path of no more learning|[sound:3-286a.mp3]\n{mi slob pa'i lam/} The Path of No More Learning\nSyllables: {mi/} = {dgag sgra/}; {slob pa/} = to train, to teach, as in {slob sbyong byed pa/} = to study; {'i/} = {'brel sgra/}; {lam/} = path, as in {skor lam/} = circumambulation path\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-286b.mp3]\n {lam lnga yi nang nas nga'i dga' shos ga re red zer na mi slob pa'i lam de red/_gang yin zer na lam de thob dus gang yang slob yag lhag yod ma red/_skyid pa la/}  If you were to ask [me] what my favorite was among the Five Paths, it would be the Path of no more learning, because if one obtains that, there is nothing left to be trained in. How fun!
to be free from|[sound:3-287a.mp3]\n{dang bral ba/} to be free from, to be separate from\nSyllables: {kha bral ba/} = to separate, to break away from; {nye ring chags sdang gnyis dang bral ba'i btang snyoms/} = equanimity which is free from attachment towards those who are close and free from aversion towards those who are distant\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-287b.mp3]\n {sems can thams cad sdug bsngal dang bral na snyam pa'i bsam pa ga re red zer na snying rje red/}  What is the thought “may all sentient beings be free from suffering”? It is compassion.
to be separate from|[sound:3-287a.mp3]\n{dang bral ba/} to be free from, to be separate from\nSyllables: {kha bral ba/} = to separate, to break away from; {nye ring chags sdang gnyis dang bral ba'i btang snyoms/} = equanimity which is free from attachment towards those who are close and free from aversion towards those who are distant\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-287b.mp3]\n {sems can thams cad sdug bsngal dang bral na snyam pa'i bsam pa ga re red zer na snying rje red/}  What is the thought “may all sentient beings be free from suffering”? It is compassion.
to clear away|[sound:3-288a.mp3]\n{sel ba/} to clear away, dispel\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-288b.mp3]\n {bar chad kun sel zer ba'i dpe cha de lta myong yod pas/_lta myong med/_yin na'i dpe cha yi mtshan thengs ma mang po go myong yod/_da de gcig lta dgos yod sa red/}  Have you ever read the book {bar chad kun sel/} (Dispell(er) of all obstacles)? - No, I’ve never read it, but I’ve heard the name of this text many times. (H) So, maybe I should read it [one day].
