thoughts which cause suffering to arise|[sound:3-324a.mp3]\n{sdug bsngal bskyed byed kyi rgyur 'gyur ba'i rnam rtog} Thoughts which cause suffering to arise, thoughts which make suffering come into being\nSyllables: {sdug bsngal/} = suffering, duḥkha; {bskyed pa/} = to give rise to, as in: {bskyed rim/} = Generation Stage; {byed pa/} = to do, verbaliser, as in: {rgyab skyor byed pa/} = to support; {kyi/} = {'brel sgra/}; {rgyu/} = cause; {ra/} = {la don/}; {'gyur ba/} = to become, as in: {mar gyur/} = have been/become (our) mothers, {bstan 'gyur/} = commentaries [of the Buddha’s Words]; {'i/} = {'brel sgra/}; {rnam rtog} = conceptual thinking, as in: {rnam rtog tsha po/} = superstitious\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-324b.mp3]\n {nyon mongs kyi mtshan nyid ni sdug bsngal bskyed byed kyi rgyur 'gyur ba'i rnam rtog ze lab na 'grig gi red pas/_'grig gi yod 'gro'o/}  Is it fine to say that the definition of afflictions is that they are thoughts which cause suffering to arise? It’s probably fine.
