to fall into the extreme of nihilism|[sound:3-329a.mp3]\n{chad mthar lhung ba/} To fall into the extreme of nihilism, the extreme of nothingness, of annihilation\nSyllables: {chad mtha'/} = view of annihilation; {chad pa/} = to be separate, as in: {thang chad pa/} = to be tired, exhausted; {mtha'/} = end, as in: {grub mtha'/} = philosophical tenet, {mtha' ma la/} = in the end; {ra/} = {la don/} = in, at, on; {lhung ba/} = to fall\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-329b.mp3]\n {nang pas las rgyu 'bras khas len gyi yod tsang chad mthar lhung gi yod ma red pa/_tag tag red/_nang chos ni rtag mtha' dang chad mtha' gnyis ka la ma lhung bar dbu ma'i lam red/}  Given Buddhists accept karma, cause and effect, they do not fall into the extreme of nihilism, right? Exactly! Buddhism is the middle way which does not fall into either of the extremes of permanence or annihilation.
