mind which wishes others to be free from suffering|[sound:3-362a.mp3]\n{gzhan sdug bsngal dang bral 'dod kyi blo/} mind [set] which wishes others to be free from suffering\nSyllables: {gzhan/} = other, as in: {bdag gzhan/} = self and other, {rang las gzhan gces/} = cherishing others more than oneself; {sdug bsngal/} = suffering, duḥkha, {sdug} = {sdug po/} = bad, poor, {bsngal/} = pain; {dang /} = {dang sgra/}; {bral ba/} = to be free from, as in: {nye ring chags sdang gnyi+i+i+is dang bral ba'i btang snyoms/} = equanimity which is free from the two – attachment to the close and aversion towards the distant ones; {'dod pa/} = to wish, want, as in: {'dod chags/} = desire, attachment; {kyi/} = {'brel sgra/}; {blo/} = mind, as in: {blo la 'dzin pa/} = to memorize, {blo gros/} = intelligence, intellect\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-362b.mp3]\n {snying rje yi mtshan nyid ga re red zer na gzhan sdug bsngal dang bral 'dod kyi blo yang dag pa gcig la go gi yod red/}  If one were to ask what the definition of compassion is, it refers to a genuine mindset which wishes for others to be free from suffering.
examples of the classifications|[sound:3-363a.mp3]\n{dbye ba'i dpe/} examples of the classifications, divisions of [the different types of bodhicitta]\nSyllables: {dbye ba/} = division, as in: {dbye ba phye ba/} = to divide; {'i/} = {'brel sgra/}; {dpe/} = example, as in: {gtam dpe/} = proverb\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-363b.mp3]\n {byang chub kyi sems kyi dbye ba'i dpe de tsho go myong yod pas/}  Have you heard the examples of the divisions of bodicitta? -Of course! They are the shepherd-like, the king-like and ferryman-like bodhicitta, correct?
