both oneself and others|[sound:3-397a.mp3]\n{rang gzhan gnyis ka} both oneself and others\nSyllables: {rang /} = I, as in: {rang btsan/} = independence; {gzhan/} = other, as in: {rang las gzhan gces/} = cherishing others more than oneself, {gzhan phan sgrub pa/} = to accomplish the benefit of others; {gnyis ka} = both, as in: {gnyis su med pa/} = non-duality\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-397b.mp3]\n {rgyal ba rin po ches ga re gsung gi yod red zer na spyir btang phan sems yang na khyad par du byang chub kyi sems bskyed na rang gzhan gnyis ka la phan gyi red ze gsung gi 'dug}  What does His Holiness the XIV Dalai Lama say? (H) If one gives rise to altruism or in particular to bodhicitta, it is of benefit to both oneself and others. (H)
the three – listening|[sound:3-398a.mp3]\n{thos bsam sgom gsum/} the three – listening, reflection and meditation, study, contemplation and meditation\nSyllables: {thos/} = to hear, listen, as in: {go thos/} = from what I heard, {nyan thos/} = śrāvaka, listener; {bsam/} = to think, reflect, as in: {bsam blo 'khor ba/} = to think involuntarily, {bsam tshul/} = opinion; {sgom/} = to meditate; {gsum/} = three, as in: {dkon mchog gsum/} = The Three Jewels\n-----\nExample: [sound:3-398b.mp3]\n {khyed rang gi bsam tshul la nang chos kyi nyams len tshang ma thos bsam sgom gsum gyi nang logs la 'dus bsdad yod red pas/_gang shes/_gtan gtan med/_yin na'i phal cher yod kyi red bsam gyi 'dug_de tsho rgya chen po 'dug}  In your opinion, are all Buddhist practices included in the three: listening, reflection and meditation? -I have got no clue, I am not sure. However, I think that most likely they must be as they [those three] are very vast.
